WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:04.605
Day one at Cymbal Retail, and the security chief hands you a list.

2
00:00:04.625 --> 00:00:06.717
Analysts can see every table.

3
00:00:06.737 --> 00:00:09.055
Card numbers sit in plain columns.

4
00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:11.990
And nobody knows who could copy the data out.

5
00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:15.400
Your job: lock it down, without slowing anyone down.

6
00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:16.700
Six ideas do it.

7
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.150
Let's take them one at a time.

8
00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:20.475
First, IAM.

9
00:00:20.495 --> 00:00:24.260
It answers one question: who can do what, on which resource?

10
00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:25.722
Think of an office building.

11
00:00:25.742 --> 00:00:27.798
A badge for every door is risky.

12
00:00:27.818 --> 00:00:30.045
A badge for one room is safe.

13
00:00:30.065 --> 00:00:35.680
Access flows down: from the organization, to folders, to projects, to each resource.

14
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.340
So a wide grant at the top cannot be undone lower down.

15
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:44.075
Basic roles, like Owner and Editor, are almost always the wrong answer.

16
00:00:44.095 --> 00:00:47.050
The safe pattern: the narrowest predefined role,

17
00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.885
on the smallest resource, given to a group.

18
00:00:49.905 --> 00:00:55.785
For an analyst, that is job user on the project, plus data viewer on one dataset.

19
00:00:55.805 --> 00:00:59.195
Next: what if people should see only part of a table?

20
00:00:59.215 --> 00:01:01.445
Use the coarsest control that works.

21
00:01:01.465 --> 00:01:04.130
Usually, that is a separate dataset.

22
00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:08.415
An authorized view shares the results, but hides the source tables.

23
00:01:08.435 --> 00:01:12.475
A row access policy shows each team only its own rows.

24
00:01:12.495 --> 00:01:16.810
A policy tag hides one column, or masks it, like a card number.

25
00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:19.985
The trap is copying data into many filtered tables.

26
00:01:20.005 --> 00:01:21.935
Never do that.

27
00:01:21.955 --> 00:01:23.647
Third idea: encryption.

28
00:01:23.667 --> 00:01:25.920
Good news: it is always on.

29
00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:28.820
So the exam never asks if data is encrypted.

30
00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:30.930
It asks who holds the key.

31
00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:32.730
By default, Google holds it.

32
00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:36.190
With CMEK, you hold it, in Cloud KMS.

33
00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:37.965
Must the key stay outside Google?

34
00:01:37.985 --> 00:01:40.025
That is Cloud EKM.

35
00:01:40.045 --> 00:01:43.860
If a rule demands hardware keys, add Cloud HSM.

36
00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:44.897
One more thing.

37
00:01:44.917 --> 00:01:48.560
Rotating a key does not re-encrypt old data.

38
00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:52.320
Fourth: personal data, like card numbers and emails.

39
00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:55.657
Sensitive Data Protection, also called Cloud DLP,

40
00:01:55.677 --> 00:01:58.830
finds it for you, across thousands of tables.

41
00:01:58.850 --> 00:01:59.755
Then it hides it.

42
00:01:59.775 --> 00:02:02.565
And how you hide it is the exam question.

43
00:02:02.585 --> 00:02:03.390
Never need it back?

44
00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:04.278
Mask it.

45
00:02:04.298 --> 00:02:05.128
Need joins?

46
00:02:05.148 --> 00:02:05.740
Hash it.

47
00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.078
Need the original later?

48
00:02:07.098 --> 00:02:09.500
Use deterministic encryption.

49
00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:12.375
And to keep a sixteen digit card looking like a card,

50
00:02:12.395 --> 00:02:15.350
use format-preserving encryption.

51
00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:17.860
Fifth: stopping data from leaving.

52
00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.148
IAM alone cannot do it.

53
00:02:20.168 --> 00:02:24.045
A valid user can still copy a table to a personal project.

54
00:02:24.065 --> 00:02:27.683
VPC Service Controls draws a wall around your projects.

55
00:02:27.703 --> 00:02:31.405
Data cannot cross it, even with valid IAM.

56
00:02:31.425 --> 00:02:33.418
Must data stay in one country?

57
00:02:33.438 --> 00:02:37.965
Use regional resources, plus the resource locations org policy.

58
00:02:37.985 --> 00:02:41.965
And remember: a BigQuery dataset's location can never change.

59
00:02:41.985 --> 00:02:44.315
You copy it instead.

60
00:02:44.335 --> 00:02:46.975
Last idea: how you lay things out.

61
00:02:46.995 --> 00:02:49.650
Give dev, test and prod their own projects.

62
00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:52.385
A mistake in dev then stays in dev.

63
00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:55.160
In BigQuery, the dataset is the security boundary.

64
00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:58.020
So: one client, one dataset.

65
00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:03.270
And before you lock anything down, read the audit logs to see who uses what.

66
00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:05.730
Let's recap, in twenty seconds.

67
00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:07.793
Give the narrowest role, to a group.

68
00:03:07.813 --> 00:03:10.030
Use the coarsest control that works.

69
00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:12.064
And know who holds the key.

70
00:03:12.084 --> 00:03:14.301
Hide PII by what you need later.

71
00:03:14.321 --> 00:03:17.039
Block leaks with VPC Service Controls.

72
00:03:17.059 --> 00:03:19.599
And split projects by environment.

73
00:03:19.619 --> 00:03:23.394
Now scroll down, and prove it at the checkpoint.
